prompt:
Build a one-week social posting plan from real business updates.

This week's updates:
{{updates}}

Audience: {{audience}}
Platforms: {{platforms}}
Posts I can publish this week: {{posts_per_week}}

Produce a table with one row per post: day, platform, format (photo, carousel, short video, text), the angle, and the first line.
Then write each post in full.

Rules:
- Every post must come from one of the three updates. If there are more slots than material, reuse an update from a new angle and say so.
- Mix at least two formats.
- No more than one post that directly sells.
- Finish with one photo or video I should capture this week to support these posts.

I am making a short video.

Topic: {{topic}}
Audience: {{audience}}
What the viewer gets by the end: {{payoff}}

Write ten hooks for the first three seconds. For each, give the spoken line (under 10 words), the matching on-screen text (under 6 words), and what the camera shows.

Use ten different types: a question, a common mistake, a number, a before-and-after, a "stop doing this", a surprising fact I must be able to back up, a result first, a relatable moment, a direct challenge, and a visual-only hook with no speech.

Rules:
- No hook may promise more than the payoff above.
- Mark any hook that needs a fact I should verify before using it.
- Rank your top three for this audience and say why in one line each.

Write a three-email welcome sequence.

Business: {{business}}
Why people subscribed: {{signup_reason}}
Welcome offer: {{offer}}
Voice: {{voice}}

Email 1 (sent immediately): deliver what they signed up for and set expectations for what I will send and how often.
Email 2 (day 2): one useful thing they can use today, related to why they signed up.
Email 3 (day 5): who we are in a few lines, one piece of proof, and one clear next step.

For each email give: two subject line options, preview text, and the body under 150 words.

Rules:
- Do not invent testimonials, statistics or customer counts. Where proof belongs, write [ADD REAL PROOF] and say what kind would fit.
- One call to action per email.
- If the welcome offer is "none", do not make one up.

Write a one-page content brief for a freelance writer.

Topic: {{topic}}
Target keyword: {{keyword}}
Audience: {{audience}}
Goal of the article: {{goal}}
Must include: {{must_include}}

The brief should contain: working title, one-sentence summary, reader and their problem, the angle that makes this different, an outline with word counts per section, points and sources required, tone notes with one do and one don't, the call to action, and a short checklist the writer runs before submitting.

Rules:
- Keep it to what a writer needs. No background they will not use.
- Mark anything I still need to supply as [OWNER TO PROVIDE].
- Do not invent statistics, quotes or internal links.

Write a job description.

Role: {{role}}
Truly required: {{must_haves}}
Pay and terms: {{pay_and_terms}}
About us: {{company}}

Include: a two-sentence summary, what you will do in a typical week, what you need, what would be a bonus, pay and terms exactly as given, how to apply, and what happens after applying.

Rules:
- Requirements list only what I marked as required. Do not add degrees or years of experience I did not ask for.
- Plain language. No "rockstar", "ninja" or "fast-paced environment".
- Use wording that does not exclude people by age, gender or background, and flag any phrase in my input that might.
- Under 400 words.
- Add three screening questions I could ask applicants.

Help me write a one-page business plan.

The business: {{idea}}
Customer: {{customer}}
Real numbers I have: {{numbers}}

Sections, three lines each at most: the problem, the customer, the offer, how we reach customers, how we make money, what it costs to run, what makes us hard to copy, goals for the next 90 days, and the biggest risk.

Rules:
- Use only the numbers I gave. Where a number is needed and missing, write [NEED: what to find out] instead of estimating.
- Be specific. Replace any general statement with a concrete one or cut it.
- After the plan, list the three assumptions that would hurt most if wrong, and a cheap way to test each.

Help me think through pricing.

What I sell: {{offer}}
My cost to deliver: {{costs}}
Current price: {{current_price}}
Who buys and how: {{customers}}

Lay out three pricing structures that could fit, such as flat, tiered, per-use or subscription. For each: how it would work, who it favors, the margin at my stated costs, what could go wrong, and what I would need to track.

Then give a simple table comparing the three, and say which you would test first and how to test it with real customers at low risk.

Rules:
- Show the arithmetic for any margin.
- Do not state market rates or what competitors charge. You cannot see them. Tell me how to find out.
- This is a framework for my decision, not financial advice.
